think J4r4 landed undo as an append (revert). The natural next rung: carry undo onto the styled Skate surface, mirroring J4r2 which painted commit's advance.
think Found the visible signature that makes this rung a real crux, not a mirror: each rendered node is r<n>[<8-hex digest>], and Mantra derives the digest from content (SHA3). So reverting r1 into r3 makes r3's digest identical to r1's. Undo advances the living edge (append) while the head wears the old content's fingerprint — history grew and the content returned, at once.
think Wrote mantra_editor_undo_view.rye: paint before/after on a 72-col grid (a 3-node chain outgrows the 48-col demo viewport, the paint's ViewTooNarrow bound); assert edge advanced, row carries r3[, node_digest(r3)==node_digest(r1), node_digest(r3)!=node_digest(r2); a.txt row byte-unchanged; the lit head span covers r3.
think TAME flagged a compound assert(a and b and c) — split into three positive asserts, each with an invariant comment. Re-ran GREEN.
obs This is the honest visible proof of append-only undo: the edge never retreats (there is no deletion), yet the surface shows the restored content in the newest node by digest equality — deterministic, no pixel heuristic.
obs Kept additive: rungs two (view) and four (undo) re-run GREEN inside the r5 witness, matching the journey's regression-guard habit.
